select m.id, 1, $L$Why Run Tests in CI$L$, $L$15 minute read$L$, $L$A suite that only runs when someone remembers to run it is a suite that quietly stops running. This lesson makes the case for continuous integration: fast, automatic feedback on every push and pull request, so your tests become a real quality gate rather than a local ritual.$L$, array[$L$Explain why running tests on every push and pull request beats running them locally by hand$L$, $L$Describe the feedback loop CI creates and why its speed matters$L$, $L$Distinguish a test that gates a merge from a test that merely exists$L$], $L$## The problem with "run it locally"
A test suite you run on your own machine has a fatal weakness: it depends on a human choosing to run it, on the right branch, with the right dependencies, before they merge. In practice that means it runs less and less often. Someone is in a hurry, the suite is slow, the branch "only changed a comment" — and the untested change lands. The tests still exist; they have simply stopped being consulted at the moment they matter.

Continuous integration removes the human from that loop. Every push and every pull request triggers the suite automatically, in a clean environment, and reports back before anyone merges. The tests run whether or not anyone remembers them.

## Fast feedback is the product
The value of CI is not that the tests run — it is that they run *quickly, automatically, and at the moment a change is proposed*. A failure reported ninety seconds after you open a pull request is cheap to fix: the change is still in your head, the branch is small, nothing else depends on it yet. The same failure discovered a week later in a shared environment is expensive — you have to reconstruct what changed, and other work has piled on top.

So the goal of a CI suite is timely, trustworthy evidence about the change in front of you. Everything else in this module — caching, artifacts, sharding — exists to keep that feedback fast and useful as the suite grows.

## A gate, not a ritual
There is a real difference between a test that *runs* and a test that *gates*. A suite that runs in CI but whose result nobody looks at, or that is allowed to be red on the main branch, is decoration. It produces a green tick that means nothing because a red one changes nothing.

A test becomes a quality gate when a failing result actually blocks something — a merge, a deploy — and the team treats red as "stop and fix", not "retry until green". CI is the mechanism that makes gating possible; branch protection (Lesson 5) is what turns the mechanism into an enforced rule.

## Green still is not "good"
CI does not change the central truth of this course. A green pipeline proves the suite you wrote passed in a clean environment on this change — no more. If the tests are weak, CI runs weak tests reliably and reports a green tick you should not trust. CI makes your suite *dependable and visible*; it does not make it *meaningful*. That part is still on you.

## Key takeaway
Running tests in CI turns a suite from something people occasionally remember into an automatic quality gate that gives fast, trustworthy feedback on every change — but CI only runs the suite you built, so a reliably green pipeline is only as valuable as the tests behind it.$L$, $L$Consider the difference between the two feedback loops for the same one-line change.

Without CI, the loop depends on discipline that erodes under deadline pressure:

~~~text
1. Developer changes a checkout total calculation.
2. Means to run the suite; it takes 8 minutes; they skip it "just this once".
3. Merges. The bug ships to the shared environment.
4. Two days later a manual tester notices totals are wrong.
5. Someone bisects three days of commits to find the cause.
~~~

With CI, the same change is caught before it ever merges:

~~~text
1. Developer opens a pull request for the checkout change.
2. GitHub Actions runs the suite automatically in a clean runner.
3. Ninety seconds later the pull request shows a red X: totals test failed.
4. Developer fixes it while the change is still fresh; pushes again; green.
5. Only then can the pull request be merged.
~~~

Nothing about the *tests* differs between the two — the same checkout total test exists in both worlds. What differs is whether it runs automatically, at the right moment, and whether its result can block the merge. That is the entire contribution of CI, and it is why the rest of this module is about keeping that automatic run fast and its evidence usable.$L$, $L$- Treating CI as "the place tests happen" while still allowing the main branch to sit red for days
- Making the pipeline so slow that people learn to ignore it or merge before it finishes
- Assuming a green tick means the change is safe, when it only means a possibly-weak suite passed
- Running tests in CI but never gating a merge on them, so the result informs nobody and blocks nothing$L$, $L$On a delivery team, the first question about any test suite is not "how many tests?" but "does it run on every pull request, and does red actually stop a merge?" A thousand tests that run nightly and are usually a bit red protect nothing — the team has learned to scroll past them. Ten solid tests that run on every push and genuinely block a merge on failure protect real behaviour. Wire the suite into CI early, keep it fast, and treat the first flaky failure as a bug in the suite to fix now, before the team starts ignoring red.$L$, $L$Take an existing Playwright suite (or a small starter one) and write down its current feedback loop honestly: when does it actually run, who looks at the result, and can a failing result stop anything from merging? Then describe the loop you want instead — trigger, audience, and consequence of red. Deliverable: a one-page before-and-after of the feedback loop, naming the specific point at which a failure should block a merge.$L$, $L$Think of a suite you have worked with that ran but rarely blocked anything. What did its green ticks actually mean to the team, and what would have had to change for a red tick to matter?$L$, $L$What is the single most important thing CI adds to a test suite that already exists locally? (Answer: it runs the suite automatically on every change, at the moment it matters, so the result can gate a merge rather than depending on someone remembering to run it)$L$, array[$L$I can explain why automatic per-change runs beat run-it-locally in practice$L$, $L$I can describe why the speed of CI feedback changes the cost of a bug$L$, $L$I can tell the difference between a test that runs and a test that gates$L$]
