select m.id, 3, $L$Installing Browsers & Caching in CI$L$, $L$17 minute read$L$, $L$A clean runner has no browsers, so Playwright cannot drive anything until you install them — with their system dependencies. This lesson covers the official `npx playwright install --with-deps` command, why `--with-deps` matters on Linux, and how caching keeps repeated installs from dominating your run time.$L$, array[$L$Install Playwright browsers and their system dependencies in CI$L$, $L$Explain why --with-deps is needed on a GitHub-hosted Linux runner$L$, $L$Use caching sensibly and know what is safe to cache and what is not$L$], $L$## Browsers are not in your node_modules
Installing the `@playwright/test` npm package does not install the browsers it drives. The browser binaries — Chromium, Firefox, WebKit — are downloaded separately into a Playwright browsers folder. On your own machine you ran `npx playwright install` once and forgot about it. A CI runner starts empty every time, so it has no browsers at all until a step installs them.

## The official command
The Playwright-recommended step for CI is:

~~~bash
npx playwright install --with-deps
~~~

Two parts matter. `install` downloads the browser binaries Playwright needs. `--with-deps` also installs the operating-system libraries those browsers depend on — fonts, graphics and media libraries a headless browser needs to run. On a bare Linux runner those libraries are not present, and without them the browsers fail to launch with cryptic shared-library errors. `--with-deps` uses the runner's package manager to install them for you, which is why it is the standard CI incantation.

To install just one browser and save time, name it:

~~~bash
npx playwright install --with-deps chromium
~~~

## Why installs are slow, and where caching fits
Downloading browsers and running `npm ci` are the two slowest parts of a typical run. Caching can help — but choose what you cache carefully.

Cache your **npm dependencies**: they are large, change only when the lockfile changes, and `actions/setup-node` has this built in. Turn it on by pointing it at your lockfile:

~~~yaml
- uses: actions/setup-node@v4
  with:
    node-version: 20
    cache: npm
~~~

That restores `node_modules`-worth of download time when your lockfile is unchanged, keyed automatically on the lockfile hash.

## Be cautious caching browsers
It is tempting to cache the Playwright browsers folder too, and you can — but it is easy to get wrong. The cache key must include the *exact* Playwright version, or a stale cache serves browsers that do not match your installed package and you get baffling failures. If you cache browsers, key the cache on the resolved Playwright version and still run `install --with-deps` on a cache miss. For many teams the browser download is fast enough that caching it is not worth the risk of a mismatched, stale cache; measure before you add that complexity.

## A green cache does not change what green means
Caching is purely a speed optimisation. It changes nothing about what your tests prove — a cached-dependency run and a fresh one execute the same suite. Its only job is to keep feedback fast so people keep watching it. Never let a caching bug quietly serve stale browsers or dependencies, though: that turns a speed trick into a source of "passes in CI, fails for real" confusion.

## Key takeaway
A clean runner has no browsers, so run `npx playwright install --with-deps` — `--with-deps` adds the OS libraries headless browsers need on Linux; cache npm dependencies via `setup-node` for speed, and cache browsers only if you key the cache on the exact Playwright version.$L$, $L$The workflow from Lesson 2, upgraded with dependency caching and the official browser install. Note the ordering and the cache key:

~~~yaml
name: Playwright Tests

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node with npm cache
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
          cache: npm            # caches npm downloads, keyed on package-lock.json

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Install Playwright browsers and OS deps
        run: npx playwright install --with-deps

      - name: Run Playwright tests
        run: npx playwright test
~~~

The `cache: npm` line is the cheap, safe win: `actions/setup-node` hashes your `package-lock.json` and restores the npm cache when it is unchanged, so `npm ci` on the next run skips re-downloading unchanged packages. The browser install stays explicit with `--with-deps` so the OS libraries are always present — this is the step you must not skip on a Linux runner. Notice what is *not* here: no hand-rolled cache of the browsers folder. That is a deliberate choice — for most suites the download is quick and a mis-keyed browser cache causes more trouble than it saves. Add it only when you have measured the install as a real bottleneck, and only with the Playwright version in the key.$L$, $L$- Running `npx playwright install` without `--with-deps` on Linux, so browsers fail to launch with missing-library errors
- Caching the browsers folder with a key that omits the Playwright version, so a stale cache serves mismatched binaries
- Caching `node_modules` directly instead of the npm cache, which is fragile across Node and OS changes — prefer `cache: npm`
- Adding caching complexity before measuring, then debugging cache bugs that cost more time than the cache ever saved$L$, $L$On a delivery team, reach for the built-in `cache: npm` in `setup-node` first and stop there until you have evidence you need more. It is one line, it is safe, and it handles the common case. Only cache browsers when a look at the run timings shows the install is genuinely dominating the job — and when you do, put the exact Playwright version in the cache key and still run `install --with-deps` on a miss. A stale, mismatched browser cache produces failures that look like product bugs and burn an afternoon before anyone suspects the cache.$L$, $L$Add `cache: npm` to your `setup-node` step and confirm the browser install uses `--with-deps`. Run the workflow twice — once with a cold cache, once warm — and record the job duration and the timing of the install steps each time. Deliverable: the updated workflow plus a short note comparing the two run times and your recommendation on whether caching the browsers folder would be worth adding for this suite.$L$, $L$Caching only makes runs faster and changes nothing about what the tests prove. So why is a caching bug that serves stale dependencies still a genuine risk to the trustworthiness of your pipeline?$L$, $L$Why does the standard CI command use `--with-deps` rather than plain `npx playwright install`? (Answer: on a bare Linux runner it also installs the OS libraries the headless browsers need to launch, which are otherwise missing)$L$, array[$L$I can install browsers and their system dependencies with npx playwright install --with-deps$L$, $L$I can enable safe npm dependency caching through setup-node$L$, $L$I can explain why a browser cache must be keyed on the exact Playwright version$L$]
