select m.id, 4, $L$Artifacts & Reports in CI$L$, $L$17 minute read$L$, $L$When a test fails in CI you cannot open a browser and look — the runner is gone the moment the job ends. Artifacts are how you get evidence back out: this lesson uploads the Playwright HTML report and, crucially, the traces on failure so you can debug a CI failure as if you had been there.$L$, array[$L$Upload the Playwright HTML report as a build artifact with actions/upload-artifact$L$, $L$Configure trace-on-failure so a red CI run captures a debuggable trace$L$, $L$Retrieve and open artifacts to diagnose a failure you did not witness$L$], $L$## The runner disappears
A CI runner is destroyed when the job finishes. Whatever the test produced — the HTML report, screenshots, traces, videos — vanishes with it unless you deliberately copy it out first. That is what a *build artifact* is: a file or folder you upload from the job so it is attached to the run and downloadable afterwards. Without artifacts, a CI failure gives you a red X and a wall of log text, and nothing you can actually inspect.

## Upload the HTML report
Playwright's HTML reporter writes a rich, browsable report to `playwright-report/`. Upload that folder as an artifact and anyone can download it from the run and open a full report — every test, every step, every error:

~~~yaml
- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
  if: ${{ !cancelled() }}
  with:
    name: playwright-report
    path: playwright-report/
    retention-days: 7
~~~

The `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}` matters: by default a step is skipped once an earlier step has failed, but the failing run is exactly when you want the report. `!cancelled()` uploads it whether the tests passed or failed, skipping only if the whole run was cancelled.

## Traces are the real prize
The HTML report tells you *what* failed. A **trace** tells you *why*, by letting you replay the failing test: a DOM snapshot at every step, the network calls, the console, the exact locator that could not be found. Configure Playwright to record a trace when a test is retried after failing:

~~~ts
// playwright.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
  use: {
    trace: 'on-first-retry',
  },
});
~~~

`on-first-retry` records a trace only when a test fails and is retried, so you pay the (small) tracing cost only on failures, not on the whole green suite. The traces land inside `playwright-report/` (and `test-results/`), so uploading the report folder brings the traces with it.

## Debugging a failure you never saw
With the report and traces uploaded, diagnosing a CI-only failure becomes concrete: download the artifact, run `npx playwright show-report ./playwright-report`, open the failed test, and click into its trace. You get the time-travel debugger against the exact run that failed on the runner — the DOM as it was, the network, the error — without ever reproducing it locally. This is the difference between "it's red and I have no idea why" and a five-minute diagnosis.

## Evidence is the point of the gate
This lesson is where CI stops being just a pass/fail light and starts being useful. A gate that only says "no" is frustrating; a gate that says "no, and here is the trace showing exactly what broke" is a tool people are glad to have. Artifacts are how automation gives back timely, actionable evidence about a failure — the whole reason the suite exists.

## Key takeaway
The runner is destroyed when the job ends, so upload the Playwright HTML report with `actions/upload-artifact` using `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}`, and set `trace: 'on-first-retry'` so failing CI runs capture a replayable trace — turning a red tick into a debuggable piece of evidence.$L$, $L$The failure-evidence setup end to end: the config that records traces, and the workflow step that uploads the report.

First, `playwright.config.ts` — retries and trace-on-failure, gated on CI:

~~~ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  // Retry flaky failures in CI, and record a trace when a test is retried.
  retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
  reporter: 'html',
  use: {
    trace: 'on-first-retry',
    screenshot: 'only-on-failure',
  },
});
~~~

Then the upload step, added after the test step in the workflow:

~~~yaml
      - name: Run Playwright tests
        run: npx playwright test

      - name: Upload Playwright report
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        if: ${{ !cancelled() }}
        with:
          name: playwright-report
          path: playwright-report/
          retention-days: 7
~~~

The config records a trace only `on-first-retry` — so green tests cost nothing extra, but any test that fails and retries leaves behind a full trace inside `playwright-report/`. The workflow step uploads that whole folder, and `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}` ensures it uploads *even though the test step failed* — without it, a red run would skip the upload and you would lose exactly the evidence you need. After a failing run, a teammate downloads the `playwright-report` artifact, runs `npx playwright show-report ./playwright-report`, opens the failed test and steps through its trace: the DOM, the network, the failing assertion, all from the run they never saw. The red X now comes with its own explanation.$L$, $L$- Uploading the report only on success, so the one time you need it — a failure — the step is skipped
- Forgetting `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}`, so an earlier failing step causes the upload step to be skipped by default
- Setting `trace: 'on'` for every test, which slows the whole suite and produces gigabytes of traces you never look at
- Uploading raw `test-results/` and being surprised it is not browsable — the HTML report in `playwright-report/` is the human-friendly view$L$, $L$On a delivery team, the artifact upload is the step that decides whether a CI failure is a five-minute fix or an afternoon of "cannot reproduce". Make trace-on-failure and report upload part of the standard workflow template from day one, not something added in a panic after the first CI-only flake. Set a sensible `retention-days` — a week is usually plenty — so you are not paying to store months of old reports. And teach the team the `show-report` command: an uploaded trace nobody knows how to open is evidence wasted.$L$, $L$Configure `trace: 'on-first-retry'` and `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0` in your Playwright config, add an `actions/upload-artifact` step for `playwright-report/` guarded with `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}`, then deliberately introduce a failing test and push. Download the artifact from the failed run, open the report, and step through the failing test's trace. Deliverable: the config and workflow changes, plus a short note describing what the trace showed you about the failure.$L$, $L$A test fails only in CI and passes every time locally. Before artifacts and traces, how would you have approached that? With the trace from the failing CI run in front of you, what changes about how quickly you can find the cause?$L$, $L$Why is `if: ${{ !cancelled() }}` important on the artifact-upload step? (Answer: a step is skipped by default once an earlier step has failed, but a failed test run is exactly when you need the report uploaded, so this condition forces the upload to run on failure too)$L$, array[$L$I can upload the Playwright HTML report as an artifact with actions/upload-artifact$L$, $L$I can configure trace-on-failure so red CI runs capture a replayable trace$L$, $L$I can download an artifact and use show-report to debug a failure I never witnessed$L$]
